{
  "title": "Pangea™ 5.0 Distal Medial Femur Plate",
  "url": "https://www.orthobullets.com/implants/423190/pangea-tm--50-distal-medial-femur-plate",
  "subtitle": "Distal Femur Fractures Updated: Aug 1 2025",
  "sections": [
    {
      "text": "Related Implants",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "The Pangea™ series contains 3 distal femur plates",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**Distal Lateral Femur**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "**3.5 Distal Medial Femur**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "**5.0 Distal Medial Femur (this topic)**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Indications",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "The Pangea Femur Plating System is indicated for the internal fixation and stabilization of femur bone fractures and osteotomies in normal and osteopenic bone, including:",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Diaphyseal, metaphyseal, epiphyseal, extra- and intra-articular fractures",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Non-unions, malunions, and deformities",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Periprosthetic fractures",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Pangea Design",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "**Variable Angle Locking Technology**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Pangea’s variable angle locking technology uses a CoCr locking screw, which is harder than the Ti6Al4V ELI plate, allowing for the screwhead’s threads to form a definitive plate-screw interface in the plate's locking hole by engaging the softer, Ti6Al4V material.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/423190/images/fee37381-4179-427b-aa75-4c758ab8429c_screenshot_2025-01-14_at_3.53.02_pm.jpg",
                  "caption": ""
                }
              ],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "This technology allows the user to aim and lock the screw into the plate within a true 30° cone of the predetermined hole trajectory. The variable angle drill guide provided with the system offers guidance with respect to the limit of the 30° cone. The locking mechanism remains functional for up to three attempts at locking screw insertion.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Circular holes that accommodate screws are universal; accepting non-locking and locking screws within a 30° cone.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "**Tapered End**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Designed for atraumatic submuscular insertion.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "**Staggered Screw Pattern**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Designed to enable surgeon to choose point of fixation and allows for placement of screws around an implant in the intramedullary canal.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/423190/images/71129025-cf1f-4086-b804-ee46daa4bebe_screenshot_2025-01-14_at_3.53.11_pm.jpg",
                  "caption": ""
                }
              ],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "**Anterior Bend Up The Shaft**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Innovative medial plate design with metadiaphyseal twist to allow for screw placement anterior to posterior.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "**Waisted Scalloped Shape**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "**2.0mm K-Wire Holes**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "To provide temporary fixation.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/423190/images/71129025-cf1f-4086-b804-ee46daa4bebe_screenshot_2025-01-14_at_3.53.11_pm.jpg",
                  "caption": ""
                }
              ],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Placement",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Plate is in the proper position when the distal end of the plate is adjacent to the medial articular margin of the trochlea and anterior to the medial epicondyle.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Plate placement designed to reduce the potential for interference with the medial collateral ligament.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Fit",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Designed with the use of SOMA: Stryker Orthopedics Modeling and Analytics",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "SOMA includes a database with CT scans from hospitals across the world and state of the art algorithms to mine the data for shape variability, bone density, and implant fit.1",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Can be used for Periprosthetic fractures around a femoral component, and extra-articular fractures where a nail cannot be used",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "5.0mm Distal Medial Femur plate may be used as medial based fixation for metadiaphyseal fractures that extend into the shaft as well as large bone defects and nonunions where enhanced fixation with a second medial plate is required in addition to a lateral plate.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Approaches",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Surgeons may use a standard medial, medial parapatellar, or medial subvastus approach to the distal medial femur.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Technical specifications",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Standard plate lengths: 151mm- 337mm (4 to 14 holes)",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Thickness: 5.0mm",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Left and right anatomic plate options",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Drill bits: 3.2mm drill bit (706406) 4.3mm drill bit (706407)",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
